# E2E Constitution — <%= projectName %>
<!-- arbiter-managed: e2e-constitution (#1817, A4) -->

**Status:** RATIFIED
**Scope:** every Playwright (or equivalent) spec under `tests/e2e/` or `e2e/`

---

## Why this exists

Every rule below was paid for in a real incident: a flaky-looking test that was
actually a real regression, a "green" suite that shipped a dead button, a smoke
run that passed against a dev server no user ever touches. Determinism is not a
style preference — it is what makes a passing suite mean something.

This file is **yours to edit**. Arbiter installs it once and never overwrites
your changes on re-run (see `docs/GOVERNANCE/FRONTEND_CONSTITUTION.md` for the
same customizable-install contract, if present in this repo).

---

## The 10 rules

1. **Selectors: testid-contract only.** No CSS class, XPath, or visible-text
   selectors. A test that breaks because a label changed is not testing
   behavior — it is testing copy.

2. **Fake clock, installed before the first navigation.** Every spec that
   touches a date/time-dependent flow installs a fake clock *before* the first
   `page.goto()`. Real wall-clock time in a spec is a race waiting to happen.

3. **Future-only dates.** Never hardcode a past date into a form or fixture
   validated by the app under test. Use a dynamic (`now + N`) or fixed
   far-future date. A past date silently rotting into "invalid" is a recurring
   failure class, not a one-off.

4. **Dedicated seed per spec.** Each spec provisions and owns its data. No
   spec reads or mutates a fixture shared with another spec — shared mutable
   state is how "flaky" tests are born.

5. **Deterministic external providers.** Any third-party or network dependency
   (payment, weather, geocoding, email, …) is stubbed with a fixed payload in
   CI. No live external call ever gates a merge.

6. **No `sleep` / no `waitForTimeout`.** Wait on an explicit condition — a
   network response, a DOM state, a testid becoming visible. A fixed sleep is
   either too short (flaky) or too long (slow), never both right.

7. **Zero-retry `@smoke` tier (INV-130, #1817 A3).** The `@smoke` tier runs
   with **zero** retries — `retryLadder({ tier: "smoke" })` force-truncates the
   ladder to a single attempt, non-bypassably. Retries hide races; `@smoke`
   exists specifically to catch them. Non-smoke tiers may still retry.

8. **Quarantine annotates, never suppresses (INV-130, #1817 A3).** A known-
   flaky test may be quarantined — owner, linked issue, and a TTL — but it
   still runs and still reports. An entry that is incomplete or past its TTL
   fails closed (`scripts/check-e2e-quarantine.mjs`).
   Quarantine is a spotlight, not a mute button.

9. **No skip without an issue and a TTL.** A bare `test.skip(...)` is a gate
   violation. Every skip carries a linked issue and an expiry — an
   unaccountable skip rots into a permanent blind spot no one remembers.

10. **Smoke runs against the built artifact.** The `@smoke` tier exercises the
    actual production build, not a dev server. A dev-server-only green suite
    can ship a build that is broken in ways the dev server never shows.

---

## Enforcement pointers

- Rules 7–8 are mechanically enforced: `scripts/check-e2e-quarantine.mjs`
  (Track-B, generated) and `DISC-e2e-quarantine`
  (`src/conformance/dimensions.ts`, Track-A).
- Rules 1–6, 9–10 are process discipline — reviewed at PR time, not (yet)
  mechanically gated. A future gate absorbing one of these into a script
  should link back here, not duplicate the rationale.
